; NextDAAD sampled-SFX DMA pre-emption fixture (2026-08-03, rev 2).
;
; Compiled by tests\build-tests.ps1 on every run; staged as the active
; GAME.DDB in sd\SFXDI\ by tests\build-tests.ps1 -SfxDi, which also
; generates the two stimulus tones with tests\audio\mktone.py (001.WAV
; 16 kHz, 002.WAV 20 kHz) and the Layer 2 corruption-detector card with
; tests\art\mkl2card.py (001.NXI, 256x128). sd\SFXDI\ is emptied and
; refilled on every -SfxDi run and holds nothing but those four files
; plus a NEXTDAAD.NEX copy of build\nextdaad.nex - copy the folder to
; the card and launch from inside it.
;
; RUN SHEET: .superpowers\sdd\sfx-di-audible-test.md. Read it before
; running - one leg is MODE-SENSITIVE and gives a false negative on
; VGA4/VGA5/VGA6.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY REV 2 EXISTS - THE VACUOUS HALF OF REV 1
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Rev 1 played a tone and hammered 40 x GFX 0 0 while the owner
; listened. It was ALSO meant to prove the picture came through
; undamaged, and it could not: the burst copied a surface that was
; never displayed. The owner's report was exact - "NO TILE LAYER
; DISTORTION, LAYER 2 NOT ACTIVE DURING TEST". There was nothing on
; screen to corrupt, so the visual result meant nothing.
;
; That matters because commit 2ef1088 did TWO things and rev 1 could
; only prove one of them:
;   nextreg $CD bit 0 = 1  PERMITS the DAC timer interrupt to pre-empt
;                          a running DMA. PROVEN by rev 1: a clean tone
;                          through the burst is only possible if
;                          ctc_isr reached the CPU mid-transfer.
;   nextreg $CC = 0        BARS the ULA frame interrupt from doing so.
;                          UNPROVEN. im2_isr's full-context path remaps
;                          MMU6/7 around aud_tick; the same commit
;                          DELETED dma_copy's di/ei, which used to bar
;                          the frame tick by masking it at the CPU. The
;                          dev guide gives $CC's bit layout
;                          (chapter-next-interrupts.tex:512-521) but
;                          states NO reset value, and this codebase had
;                          never written the register before 2ef1088.
;
; The video passes B and C do NOT cover this. video.asm's own kernels
; (vid_copy_dma :1595, vid_fill_dma :1570) still carry di/ei around
; their arms AND their transfers, so the frame ISR cannot reach the CPU
; during a video DMA whatever $CC says. dma_copy (overlay2.asm:562) is
; the ONLY transfer in the tree that runs with interrupts live, so it is
; the only place the $CC permission is load-bearing - and location
; picture drawing is the only shipped thing that reaches it.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS FIXTURE PUTS ON SCREEN, AND WHY EACH PIECE IS THERE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CARD. PICTURE 1 + DISPLAY 0 loads 001.NXI - a 256x128
; generated card (tests\art\mkl2card.py) of flat colour bands, a 16-step
; ramp, a 2x2 checkerboard, a solid white block and a caption. gfx_blit
; ends with l2_enable, so the card IS Layer 2 coming up: if you can read
; "L2 DMA TEST" the layer is live and the burst below has something to
; damage. If you cannot, STOP - see "IF THE CARD NEVER APPEARS" in the
; run sheet. The card uses palette indices 0..15 only and NEVER index
; 255, which l2_palette_load reserves as the sole transparent entry, so
; any transparency inside the card is damage rather than art.
;
; THE RED BACKDROP. WINDOW 2 paints a solid red tilemap block at rows
; 5..18, columns 12..67 - wholly INSIDE the area the card covers, with
; margin. Layer 2 sits above the tilemap (l2_enable sets NR $15 = S L U),
; so with an intact card the red is completely hidden. Red showing
; through is a byte the copy did not write: l2_clear_back pre-fills the
; back surface with $FF = transparent before every blit, so an unwritten
; destination is a hole, not stale pixels. That turns the weakest part
; of a visual check - noticing a few wrong bytes - into a binary one.
; Geometry: Layer 2 in 256x192 mode is inset 32 px from the tilemap
; origin on both axes (chapter-next-tilemap.tex:18,
; chapter-next-layer2.tex:308), so the 128-row card covers tilemap rows
; 4..19 and columns 8..71. The text window sits at rows 20..31, clear of
; it; the header at rows 0..3, above it.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PERSISTENCE PROBLEM, AND HOW THE BURST ANSWERS IT
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; DISPLAY 0 re-renders the whole surface from the pristine cached
; source and flips. A damaged render is therefore replaced by the next
; clean one: damage NEVER accumulates on this path and a rare fault is
; a sub-frame flash. That is the shipped path and it must be exercised,
; but on its own it is a poor detector. So the burst is three phases:
;
;   2/5  24 x DISPLAY 0, back to back. The real scenario - a location
;        picture drawing under a playing effect - at the highest rate
;        the condact allows. Each render holds ~75 ms (4 frames), which
;        is watchable for a band-sized artifact but not for a speck.
;        JUDGED LIVE.
;   3/5  4 x (DISPLAY 0 + PAUSE). Same path, each render HELD half a
;        second so a single damaged render cannot flash past. The cost
;        of the hold is that the DMA duty cycle drops, which is why it
;        is a separate phase and not the whole burst.
;   4/5  20 x (GFX 0 1 + GFX 0 0). THE ACCUMULATOR. GFX 0 1 copies
;        front->back, GFX 0 0 copies back->front, both through
;        l2_copy_back_front -> dma_copy. Each copy's SOURCE is the
;        previous copy's DESTINATION, so every damaged byte is carried
;        forward into every later copy and into the final image. Damage
;        adds up over the whole phase instead of being overwritten, and
;        the result stays on the visible surface indefinitely after the
;        burst ends. Sensitivity scales with the iteration count rather
;        than with the instantaneous fault rate, and the verdict is read
;        off a still image at leisure instead of caught in flight.
;        It is also the densest DMA in the fixture - 768 dma_copy calls
;        per iteration at ~73% of wall time inside a transfer, against
;        ~10% for a DISPLAY - so it carries the audio readout too.
;
; Phase 4 is seeded by phase 3's last render, so damage from that render
; is carried into the accumulation as well.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CONTROL LEG - RUNS ONCE, AT BOOT, BEFORE ANY SOUND
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; im2_isr only remaps MMU6/7 on its full-context path, which is gated on
; audEnable - zero from main.asm until the first sample or song, and
; STICKY once set (interrupts.asm:338). -SfxDi stages no .AKY, so at
; boot, before any SFX, the frame ISR is provably MMU-free and the
; hazard under test cannot exist. The control leg runs the IDENTICAL
; burst (the same PROCESS 10 table - not a copy of it) in that state.
;
; A clean control followed by a damaged tone leg isolates exactly one
; variable. A control that is ITSELF damaged means the copy path has an
; unrelated defect and nothing below it can be read as an answer about
; $CC. The control CANNOT be repeated without a reboot: audEnable never
; goes back to zero, so any later run of it would silently not be a
; control. That is why there is no verb for it.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TONE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; tests\audio\mktone.py: 48000 bytes (exactly the 48K reserved audio
; floor, banks 25-27, so the load cannot fail on pool pressure), 8-bit
; unsigned mono, full-scale 440 Hz, centred on 128 = DAC_SILENCE, a
; whole number of cycles end to end (1320 at 16 kHz, 1056 at 20 kHz).
; Played with SFX n 2 = LOOPED: aud_smp_copy rewinds the source mid-copy
; and the ring never sees the seam, so the tone runs for as long as the
; leg needs and there is no run-out to mistake for a result. A missing
; WAV is a documented silent no-op (h_sfx falls through to the AY-effect
; path, and no GAME.SFB is staged) - so silence is a STAGING miss, not
; an audio result.
;
; NO MUSIC. The AKY player holds interrupts off ~5500 T every frame
; while a song plays, a CONTINUOUS ~0.67% rate error at 16 kHz that
; would sit under both reference phases and the burst alike. -SfxDi
; stages no .AKY at all, and sd\SFXDI\ holds nothing it did not stage.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; EVERY PHASE BOUNDARY IS A KEYPRESS PAUSE (rev 2a, owner request)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The five phases used to run back to back with only short timed holds,
; so a symptom could not be attributed to the phase that produced it.
; There is now an ANYKEY at EVERY boundary - before phase 1, between
; each pair of phases, and after the last one - each preceded by a MES
; naming the phase that just ended and the one that follows.
;
; The pauses are strictly INTERSTITIAL. No burst is interrupted: every
; ANYKEY sits between process-table entries, outside the 24-DISPLAY run,
; outside the four held renders and outside the 20 GFX pairs, so each
; burst still runs uninterrupted at full speed - that rate IS the load
; under test. Phase count, order, iteration counts, verbs and staging
; are untouched.
;
; ANYKEY, NOT "PAUSE 0". Under DAAD V3, PAUSE 0 is GETKEY (h_pause's V3
; arm, overlay0.asm) - but build-tests.ps1 compiles THIS fixture with a
; plain "DRF.exe zx next" and no -v3, so its header version is 2 and
; PAUSE 0 would mean 256 frames, not a key. ANYKEY (opcode 24, ZERO
; parameters) is the V2 wait-for-key condact, it is what the interpreter
; has always implemented (h_anykey -> wait_key_timeout), and having no
; operand it is immune to the DRC duration rescaling below. Making the
; fixture -v3 to get GETKEY would have changed the DDB version byte and
; the SYNONYM/attribute semantics of a fixture whose whole value is that
; only one variable moves - far larger and riskier than one condact.
;
; The tone keeps playing across a pause (SFX n 2 is looped and the DAC
; is interrupt-fed), which is deliberate: a boundary is also a chance to
; hear whether the tone survived the phase. The control leg's boundaries
; are still silent - ANYKEY touches no audio state and audEnable stays 0
; until the first SFX, which is issued after the control leg has run.
;
; SYSMESS 16 carries a trailing #n below. h_anykey prints it before
; blocking, and without the newline the next phase caption would be
; printed on the same line as "Press any key.".
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; PAUSE IS SCALED BY DRC
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; drb.php multiplies every PAUSE by getBaseLength/DEFAULT_NOTE_DURAION
; = 120/200 = 0.6 for the ZX NEXT target, so the authored PAUSE 63
; reaches the interpreter as 38 frames (0.76 s at 50 Hz) and the
; authored PAUSE 40 as 24 frames (0.48 s - chosen because 40 x 0.6 is
; exactly 24, with no rounding to argue about). Do not "correct" either
; number here. build-tests.ps1 asserts both compiled bytes, along with
; the 24-DISPLAY run, the 20-pair GFX run and all eight ANYKEY sites.

; System flags used by name (DAAD BLANK template spellings)
#define fPlayer             38
#define fDarkF              28

/CTL
_
/VOC
NORTH  2  noun
SOUTH  3  noun
LOOK   4  verb
T16    60 verb
T20    61 verb
HUSH   62 verb
/STX
/0  "It's too dark to see anything."
/1  "I can also see:"
/2  "What now?"
/3  "What next?"
/4  "What should I do now?"
/5  "What should I do next?"
/6  "I didn't understand."
/7  "I can't go in that direction."
/8  "I can't do that."
/9  "I have with me:"
/10 "I am wearing:"
/11 " "
/12 "Are you sure?"
/13 "Another go?"
/14 " "
/15 "OK."
/16 "Press any key.#n"
/17 " "
/18 " "
/19 " "
/20 " "
/21 " "
/22 " "
/23 "I'm not wearing one of those."
/24 "I can't. My hands are full."
/25 "I already have the _."
/26 "There isn't one of those here."
/27 "I can't carry any more things."
/28 "I don't have one of those."
/29 "I'm already wearing the _."
/30 "Y"
/31 "N"
/32 "More..."
/33 ">"
/34 " "
/35 " "
/36 "I now have the _."
/37 "I'm now wearing the _."
/38 "I've removed the _."
/39 "I've dropped the _."
/40 "I can't wear the _."
/41 "I can't remove the _."
/42 "I can't remove the _. My hands are full."
/43 "The _ weighs too much for me."
/44 "The _ is in the"
/45 "The _ isn't in the"
/46 ", "
/47 " and "
/48 ".#n"
/49 "I don't have the _."
/50 "I'm not wearing the _."
/51 "."
/52 "There isn't one of those in the"
/53 "Nothing."
/54 " "
/55 " "
/56 " "
/57 "I/O Error"
/58 " "
/59 "File name error."
/60 "Type in name of file."
/MTX
/0 "NEXTDAAD - LAYER 2 DMA PRE-EMPTION FIXTURE"
/OTX
/0 "a brass lamp"
/LTX
/0 ""
/1 "The listening room."
/CON
/0
NORTH 1
/1
SOUTH 0
/OBJ
/0    1   1   _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _   _ _
/PRO 0
> _ _   AT 0
        PROCESS 6
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        DESC @fPlayer
> _ _   PROCESS 1
/PRO 1
> _ _   PARSE 0
        REDO
> _ _   PROCESS 5
        ISDONE
        REDO
> _ _   MOVE fPlayer
        CLS
        RESTART
> _ _   SYSMESS 8
        REDO
/PRO 5
; ---- the shipped-rate leg (001.WAV, 16000 Hz) ----
> T16 _   WINDOW 1
          CLS
          MES "LEG 16 kHz - 001.WAV, the shipped rate#n"
          PROCESS 9
          SFX 1 2
          PROCESS 7
          DONE
; ---- the published-ceiling leg (002.WAV, 20000 Hz) ----
; 20000 Hz is AUD_RATE_MAX - what README.md and authoring-kit\SETUP.md
; publish as supported and what a DAAD-DOS SOUNDS set drops in at. It
; is NOT a rate anything in this project ships.
> T20 _   WINDOW 1
          CLS
          MES "LEG 20 kHz - 002.WAV, README's maximum#n"
          PROCESS 9
          SFX 2 2
          PROCESS 7
          DONE
; ---- panic stop ----
> HUSH _  MES "tone stopped#n"
          SFX 0 5
          DONE
/PRO 7
; A tone leg: reference, the shared burst, reference. Each MES lands
; immediately before the phase it names, and every phase boundary is an
; ANYKEY - the card is settled at each one, so what is on screen belongs
; to the phase just named as finished. The tone is already looping when
; the first pause is reached (SFX was issued before this process), so
; the opening boundary is also where a clean starting tone is confirmed.
> _ _   MES "CARD UP + TONE RUNNING - KEY FOR PHASE 1/5#n"
        ANYKEY
        MES "1/5 REF - steady 440 Hz, no graphics#n"
        PAUSE 63
        MES "PHASE 1/5 DONE - PRESS A KEY FOR PHASE 2/5#n"
        ANYKEY
        PROCESS 10
> _ _   MES "5/5 REF - steady 440 Hz again#n"
        PAUSE 63
        SFX 0 5
        MES "leg done - JUDGE THE HELD CARD NOW#n"
        MES "PHASE 5/5 DONE - PRESS A KEY TO FINISH#n"
        ANYKEY
/PRO 8
; The control leg. Runs ONCE, from PRO 6, before any SFX has been
; issued - so audEnable is still 0, im2_isr takes its MMU-free fast
; path, and the hazard under test cannot occur. Same PROCESS 10 burst
; as the tone legs, byte for byte.
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        CLS
        MES "CONTROL - same burst, NO sound. The frame ISR#n"
        MES "cannot remap MMU while audEnable is 0.#n"
        PROCESS 9
        MES "CONTROL: CARD UP - KEY FOR PHASE 2/5#n"
        ANYKEY
        PROCESS 10
> _ _   MES "CONTROL DONE. The card above is your reference:#n"
        MES "damage HERE is not about the interrupt fix.#n"
        PAUSE 63
        MES "PRESS A KEY TO START THE 16 kHz TONE LEG#n"
        ANYKEY
/PRO 9
; Bring the card up. PICTURE is a CONDITION, so a failed load aborts
; this entry and the next one reports it instead of the fixture
; silently testing nothing - which is exactly how rev 1 failed.
> _ _   PICTURE 1
        DISPLAY 0
        DONE
> _ _   MES "*** PICTURE 1 FAILED - no 001.NXI staged.#n"
        MES "*** STOP. Nothing below is a result.#n"
        DONE
/PRO 10
; THE SHARED BURST - phases 2, 3 and 4. Called by both the control leg
; and every tone leg, so the two runs differ in exactly one thing:
; whether a sample is playing.
;
; Each entry opens with the boundary pause that CLOSES the previous
; phase, so the burst runs never have a pause inside them. The boundary
; INTO phase 2 belongs to the caller (the control leg and the tone legs
; word it differently), and the boundary out of phase 4 is the last
; entry here.
> _ _   MES "2/5 BURST - 24 x DISPLAY 0 (watch it live)#n"
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
        DISPLAY 0
> _ _   MES "PHASE 2/5 DONE - PRESS A KEY FOR PHASE 3/5#n"
        ANYKEY
        MES "3/5 HELD - 4 x DISPLAY 0, each held 0.5 s#n"
        DISPLAY 0
        PAUSE 40
        DISPLAY 0
        PAUSE 40
        DISPLAY 0
        PAUSE 40
        DISPLAY 0
        PAUSE 40
> _ _   MES "PHASE 3/5 DONE - PRESS A KEY FOR PHASE 4/5#n"
        ANYKEY
        MES "4/5 ACCUMULATE - 20 x GFX 0 1 + GFX 0 0#n"
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
        GFX 0 1
        GFX 0 0
> _ _   MES "PHASE 4/5 DONE - JUDGE THE CARD, PRESS A KEY#n"
        ANYKEY
/PRO 6
; Screen layout. Layer 2 (256x128 card) covers tilemap rows 4..19 and
; columns 8..71, so:
;   WINDOW 0  rows 0..3    header, always above the card
;   WINDOW 2  rows 5..18   the red backdrop, wholly under the card
;   WINDOW 1  rows 20..31  the running text, always below the card
> _ _   WINDOW 0
        WINAT 0 0
        WINSIZE 4 80
        PAPER 0
        INK 7
        CLS
        MESSAGE 0
> _ _   WINDOW 2
        WINAT 5 12
        WINSIZE 14 56
        PAPER 2
        INK 6
        CLS
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        WINAT 20 0
        WINSIZE 12 127
        PAPER 0
        INK 7
        CLEAR fDarkF
        CLS
        MES "The red block is BEHIND Layer 2. An intact#n"
        MES "card hides it completely. Red showing through#n"
        MES "the card is a byte the copy did not write.#n"
        MES "T16 / T20 repeat a leg, HUSH stops the tone.#n"
        MES "Run sheet: sfx-di-audible-test.md#n"
> _ _   PROCESS 8
; The 16 kHz leg then runs once, automatically, so the primary readout
; needs no typing. Same shape as T16 in /PRO 5 - the verb table cannot
; be reused here because no verb has been parsed yet.
> _ _   WINDOW 1
        CLS
        MES "AUTO-RUN: 16 kHz leg#n"
        PROCESS 9
        SFX 1 2
        PROCESS 7
        GOTO 1
/END
